{"slug": "rc-low-pass-high-pass-filter", "targets": ["rc low pass filter", "cutoff frequency high pass filter", "f 1 2pirc"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">RC Low Pass Filter Cutoff Values for Common R and C Pairs</h2><p>An <strong>rc low pass filter</strong> passes signals below its cutoff and attenuates those above it, with the cutoff given by f = 1/2πRC. The cutoff frequency high pass filter formula is exactly the same, so one table of standard R and C pairs serves both circuits.</p><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">f = 1/2πRC Quick Table</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">R</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">C</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Cutoff frequency fc</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 nF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">15.9 kHz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4.7 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 nF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3.39 kHz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 nF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1.59 kHz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 nF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1.59 kHz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 nF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">159 Hz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 µF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">159 Hz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 µF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">15.9 Hz</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 kΩ</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 µF</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1.59 Hz</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Gain at Different Frequencies</h3><p>For a first order low pass stage the gain is 1 / √(1 + (f/fc)²) and the phase is minus arctan(f/fc). The high pass gain is (f/fc) / √(1 + (f/fc)²).</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">f / fc</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Low pass gain</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">High pass gain</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.995 (about 0 dB)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.0995 (minus 20 dB)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.707 (minus 3 dB)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.707 (minus 3 dB)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.0995 (minus 20 dB)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.995 (about 0 dB)</td></tr></table></div><p>A single RC stage rolls off at 20 dB per decade, so cascade buffered stages if a steeper slope is needed.</p>"}